hopf-rinow.definitionEn:
Let $(M,g)$ be a **connected** Riemannian manifold and let $d$ be the Riemannian distance, i.e. the infimum of lengths of piecewise smooth curves joining the two points. The **Hopf–Rinow theorem** states that the following four conditions are **equivalent**:

① $(M,d)$ is **complete as a metric space** (every Cauchy sequence converges inside $M$);
② $M$ is **geodesically complete** (every maximal geodesic extends to a geodesic defined for all $t\in\mathbb R$);
③ there exists a point $p$ such that $\exp_p$ is defined on all of $T_pM$ (by the theorem this holds at one point iff it holds at every point);
④ every **closed bounded subset** of $M$ is compact (equivalently, every closed ball $\overline B(p,r)$ is compact).

If any one holds (hence all hold), then **any two points $p,q$ are joined by a minimizing geodesic** $\gamma$ with $L(\gamma)=d(p,q)$ — a genuinely shortest curve. Existence is not uniqueness: on $S^2$ antipodal points are joined by infinitely many minimizing geodesics.

The deepest point is ① $\iff$ ②: metric completeness (an analytic condition) is the same as extending geodesics forever (a geometric one). The implication ① $\Rightarrow$ ② is comparatively easy, while ② $\Rightarrow$ ① is the hard core, proved through the existence of minimizing geodesics. Note that ④ fails in general metric spaces: the closed unit ball of $\ell^2$ is complete and bounded but not compact; complete Riemannian manifolds are exactly the metric spaces in which closed bounded sets are compact.

Completeness cannot be dropped: on $M=\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}$ the points $(1/k,0)$ form a Cauchy sequence without limit, and between $p=(-1,0)$ and $q=(1,0)$ there is no minimizing geodesic, since $d(p,q)=2$ but every connecting curve has length $>2$ (semicircles around the origin give lengths tending to $2$).
